WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:09.680
One of the best ways of gathering data for date&nbsp;
science projects is through the use of APIs. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:14.960
API stands for Application Programming Interface&nbsp;
and it's something that allows computer programs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:20.560
to talk to each other. There's a lot of them that&nbsp;
operate within your operating system directly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.200
but it's also a way of accessing web data and&nbsp;
pulling in data live from multiple sources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.760
The most common API used in data science is going&nbsp;
to be what is called a REST API where REST stands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:37.040
for Representational State Transfer and that's the&nbsp;
software architectural style of the worldwide web.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:43.760
What we're going to do with REST APIs is&nbsp;
access data on a webpage via HTTP and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:49.040
data that we get is usually going to be in the&nbsp;
JSON or Java Script Object Notation format.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.800
What's nice about this is that by using this&nbsp;
REST API, we can send data directly from that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:01.040
webpage into other programs for analysis.&nbsp;
Also, REST APIs are language agnostic.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:08.720
Some of the most popular APIs are Social APIs.&nbsp;
Facebook for example has information like that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.200
Twitter has a very common API and&nbsp;
you can get data from Twitter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:21.040
Google Talk is one of the most popular. Foursquare&nbsp;
and SoundCloud are also very popular social APIs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.840
You can build applications that connect&nbsp;
with these social networks directly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:33.600
In some situations, you can pull the data in for a&nbsp;
data science project. Also, very common are visual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.840
APIs such as Google Maps or Youtube or AccuWeather&nbsp;
is very common, and so is Pinterest and Flickr.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.640
Let me give you a brief example of&nbsp;
a very, very simple API through R.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:51.680
I'm going to get some data from a website called&nbsp;
Ergast, which is for automobile racing data. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:58.240
here is what you see if you go to Ergast.com. It's&nbsp;
not much but if you click on that link, it'll take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:04.080
you to the developer API that has information&nbsp;
about Formula One races from 1950 to 2014.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:10.000
All you have to do is create a URL. I'm&nbsp;
going to do a particular search for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:18.080
people who won in 1957. This is my data in JSON&nbsp;
format, the people who won each race in 1957.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.840
Now that I know what the URL is, I can go to R.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:27.440
I'm going to use a package called jsonlite,&nbsp;
which is a way of accessing JSON data from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:33.520
web along with its dependency, curl, which is for&nbsp;
URLs. If you don't have those installed already,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:37.760
you'll want to use install packages and I'll&nbsp;
use require here to get jsonlite loaded.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:45.040
It loads curl by default. Then what I'm going to&nbsp;
do is I take that URL from earlier, which is right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:51.840
here, and I use the command from JSON and feed&nbsp;
it into an object called F1 for Formula One. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.800
if you want to see what's in there, it's a&nbsp;
list object. And so that means that it's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:03.840
big and just lots and lots of text. But it is&nbsp;
structured the same way we saw earlier. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.080
I'm going to pull up the structure here and this&nbsp;
is a way of indicating the nested structure.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:13.040
But what I'm going to do is I'm going to get&nbsp;
a piece of that list from the overall object&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:17.600
F1 and I'm going to go down a level to&nbsp;
MR data to another level, race tables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:23.680
and so on until I get to driver and I'm going to&nbsp;
feed that into an object called driver. And then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:29.120
I'll get the column names for that. There's seven&nbsp;
columns, I'm going to take four of them and put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:33.360
them in a different order and then here are&nbsp;
the first five drivers listed in this data set.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:39.280
We have the first name, the last name, Juan&nbsp;
Fangio who won the first race from Argentina,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:44.560
one of the greatest drivers ever. And so what&nbsp;
we have here is wonderful way of accessing this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:49.280
structured JSON data from the web and feeding&nbsp;
it into R so we can use it in our own analyses.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:53.840
In conclusion, APIs make&nbsp;
structured web data easy to get.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:00.000
You can get that data and put it directly&nbsp;
into the program you're using for analysis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.320
APIs are one of the best tools for data&nbsp;
science in terms of retrieving data that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:11.760
structured and can be incorporated into your&nbsp;
analysis to get the insights that you need.

